.TH UNIQ 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
uniq \- report or filter out repeated adjacent lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uniq
.RB [ \-cdiuDz ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR fields ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR chars ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR chars ]
.RB [ \-\-all\-repeated [ =\c
.IR method ]]
.RI [ input
.RI [ output ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uniq
reads
.I input
(standard input if omitted or
.BR \- )
and writes one copy of each group of adjacent identical lines to
.I output
(standard output if omitted).
Only adjacent lines are compared, so input is normally sorted first.
.PP
Lines may be arbitrarily long and may contain NUL bytes; comparison uses
the full byte length of each line.
.PP
Short options may be combined, and the argument of
.BR \-f ,
.B \-s
and
.B \-w
may be attached
.RB ( \-f2 )
or given as the next argument.
Option processing stops at the first argument that does not begin with
.BR \- ,
at a lone
.BR \- ,
or after
.BR \-\- .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Precede each output line with the number of times it occurred, in a
field four characters wide followed by a space.
.TP
.B \-d
Output only lines that are repeated.
.TP
.B \-u
Output only lines that are not repeated.
Given together,
.B \-d
and
.B \-u
suppress all output.
.TP
.B \-i
Compare lines case-insensitively.
.TP
.BI \-f " fields"
Ignore the first
.I fields
fields of each line when comparing.
A field is a run of non-blank characters preceded by blanks (spaces or
tabs); blanks following the skipped fields are also skipped.
.TP
.BI \-s " chars"
Ignore the first
.I chars
characters when comparing, after any fields skipped by
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BI \-w " chars"
Compare at most
.I chars
characters, after the skips above.
.TP
.B \-D
Print every line of each repeated group, and nothing for lines that are
not repeated.
Overrides
.BR \-d " and " \-u .
With
.BR \-c ,
each line is prefixed with its position within the group.
.TP
.BR \-\-all\-repeated [ =\c
.IR method ]
Like
.BR \-D ,
with
.I method
controlling the separator line (an empty line) between groups:
.B none
(the default) prints no separators,
.B separate
and
.B prepend
print one before every group except the first, and
.B append
prints one after every group.
.TP
.B \-z
Lines are terminated by NUL instead of newline, on both input and
output.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard error and exit with status 2.
.PP
Counts given to
.BR \-f ,
.B \-s
and
.B \-w
must be non-negative decimal integers.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A file could not be opened, or a read or write error occurred.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid option or count,
.BR \-\-help ,
or an unknown
.B \-\-all\-repeated
method.
.SH EXAMPLES
Count occurrences of each word, most frequent first:
.EX
$ tr \-s ' ' '\en' < file | sort | uniq \-c | sort \-rn
.EE
.PP
Show duplicated lines ignoring the first field:
.EX
$ sort \-k2 list | uniq \-d \-f 1
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tr (1),
.BR wc (1)
.SH STANDARDS
The
.BR \-c ", " \-d ", " \-u ", " \-f " and " \-s
options follow POSIX.1-2024.
.B \-i
follows BSD;
.BR \-D ", " \-w ", " \-z " and " \-\-all\-repeated
follow GNU.
